  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1231 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nThe mid-level ridge that has been dominating our weather pattern \nwill remain in place until early next week. Generally, it will \ncontinue to be hot and dry, although an isolated shower or \nthunderstorm promoted by the sea breeze cannot be ruled out. On the \nother hand, most locations will remain dry through Monday. \n\nAttention then shifts to an approaching mid-level trough, associated \nfront and its effects starting midweek next week. While some \ndiscrepancies remain, model guidance is beginning to come into \nagreement regarding timing and impacts towards South Texas. Overall, \nmodels suggest a mid-level low will approach Texas whilst \ninteracting with the remnants of Hurricane Polo as it approaches \nWest Texas. Initially, there was uncertainty about how much Polo \nwould affect the mid-level low, but now, confidence has increased \nas recent guidance favors the mid-level low absorbing Polo's \nremnant moisture/circulation, rather than Polo substantially \naltering the low's track. Confidence is the exact evolution \nremains somewhat limited, however, and changes in the interaction \ncould affect rainfall timing and amounts across South Texas. \n\nAs the front makes its way into Texas, model guidance suggests it \nwill temporarily stall over West/Central Texas around midweek. As it \nstalls, the front will draw moisture from the Gulf, meaning \nincreased PWAT values for South Texas, with values approaching 2.2-\n2.4\", according to the GFS, across the CWA. With the increased \nmoisture and sufficient instability, rain chances will increase \nstarting Tuesday eventually becoming more widespread as the week \ngoes on. Additionally, the aforementioned PWAT values are \nsupportive of heavy rainfall, which could lead to localized \nflooding. Confidence is not particularly high due to the lack of a\nlifting mechanism as the front stalls, but an outflow boundary \nfrom the northern storms making its way south could help with \nincreased convection in addition to the seabreeze. Ultimately, we \nare still 5 days out from the expected impacts and model guidance \ncould still change, but overall, chances for rain will increase \nsignificantly starting midweek. \n\nThrough early next week, temperatures will stay above normal. \nIncreasing cloud cover in addition to increased convection from the \naforementioned front in the latter half of the week will help \nkeep temperatures down, but it will still be warm and humid. \nAfternoon highs will drop from around the mid to upper 90's to \nlevels closer to 90 and the mid 80's. A moderate to major heat \nrisk persists through Wednesday, becoming minor by the end of the \nweek as the increased cloud coverage and convection make their way\ninto the area.\n\nRegarding minor coastal flooding, the P-ETSS estimates tide levels \nup to 1.8 feet above MSL beginning Tuesday during high tide. There \nseems to be an upward trend through midweek regarding the levels, \nbut we will monitor the trend to see if it continues and may issue \nproducts as necessary.",
